  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A wave of low pressure along a weakening frontal \nboundary brings scattered showers and storms to the area this \nafternoon into tonight. There is a Marginal Risk of severe storms \nacross much of the forecast area this afternoon.\n\nThis afternoon, a weak, diffuse frontal boundary is located from \nroughly central VA over to the Middle Peninsula. South of the \nboundary, a very hot and humid airmass is in place with temperatures \nranging from the upper 80s to mid 90s and dewpoints in the mid to \nupper 70s. The hot temperatures combined with the high humidity have \nallowed for heat index values to climb to ~100 to 105 degrees, with \nreadings of 105+ across northeast North Carolina where a Heat \nAdvisory remains in effect until 7 PM. \n\nConvective initiation has started along and just north of the \nboundary where we have already seen a couple of severe storms, one \nnear Newport News and one just south of Salisbury, MD. Strong \ndiurnal heating has destabilized the airmass, with MLCAPE \nvalues ~2000-3000 J/kg south of the boundary. Deep-layer shear \nis fairly weak along and south of the boundary (~20 to 25 knots)\nand strongest across the far northeast (~35 to 40 knots). \nExpect mainly pulse activity with potentially a few bowing \nsegments as we head through the afternoon/evening. Damaging wind\ngusts of 50 to 60 mph are possible with the strongest storms. \nSPC has a majority of the area in a Marginal (1 out of 5 risk) \nfor severe storms this afternoon. In addition, high moisture \ncontent (PWATs 2.0+\") will yield high rain rates and local urban\nor poor drainage flooding. Any storms push SSE into North \nCarolina this evening before gradually tapering off after ~02z \n(10 PM). \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonable on Saturday, becoming more unsettled \nagain for Sunday. \n\nSurface high pressure north of the Great Lakes ridges south into the \nMid-Atlantic on Saturday, pushing the surface boundary off to our \nS/SW. Light E-NE flow will usher in a slightly drier and more \nseasonable airmass with highs in the upper 80s. Most areas stay dry \non Saturday, but isolated to scattered showers/storms will be \npossible in the afternoon, especially near the NC border. On Sunday, \nthe expansive ridge over the Southern US begins to break down as a \ntrough digs over the Midwest. Return southerly flow brings the heat \nand tropical moisture back into the region. Scattered to widespread \nafternoon/evening thunderstorms return for Sunday. Thunderstorms \nwill again have the potential to produce strong to severe wind \ngusts, with SPC placing the entire forecast area in a Day 3 Marginal \n(1 out of 5) risk for severe storms. Locally heavy rainfall will \nalso be possible with any of these storms and isolated instances of \nflooding, especially in urban or poor drainage areas, will be \npossible. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Hot and humid with afternoon storms Monday ahead of \nthe next cold front.\n\nHot, humid conditions likely peak on Monday ahead of an approaching \ncold front. Highs in the low to mid 90s, combined with elevated \nhumidity will likely necessitate additional Heat Advisories for\nat least our southern/southeastern counties. Scattered showers \nand storms are also expected Monday afternoon/evening along the \nfrontal boundary. Drier and slightly cooler conditions arrive \nTuesday into midweek.",
